
Problem 1: Manual Regex & String Surgery to Rescue Broken JSON
The Exact Proof in Your Code:
In api/src/services/ai/llm.service.ts:
ts
const body = {
  model: modelName,
  messages: [ ... ],
  temperature: opts.temperature ?? 0,
  max_tokens: opts.maxTokens ?? 5000,
};
There is no response_format specified.
In llm.service.ts: Lines 104–161 contain ~60 lines of manual string manipulation:
Line 116–119: Counts unescaped quotes with regex and manually appends " if odd (cleaned += '"').
Line 121–125: Strips trailing commas and patches LaTeX backslashes.
Line 132–160: Loops character-by-character maintaining a braceStack counter, and on line 159 writes: cleaned += "}".repeat(braceStack); to force-close truncated JSON!
The Real-World Bug: Whenever a model slightly modifies its output (e.g., adds conversational text before the JSON, or truncates mid-array), your manual string surgery attempts to "guess" where braces close. When it guesses wrong, JSON.parse crashes with a fatal error: CRITICAL: LLM returned invalid JSON.
How to Solve It:
Add response_format: { type: "json_object" } directly inside the request payload body in llm.service.ts.
Because NewAPI and models like Groq, Gemini, and DeepSeek support native JSON Mode, the model's output decoder is constrained to emit strictly valid JSON.
You can safely delete the fragile 60 lines of brace-counting and quote-patching logic.
